No eslint/prettier config, node_modules. Line-length not enforced. I'll merge the import in steer-stream into one statement, matching mail-visible's single-line-ish style. I'll use the exact form the reviewer suggested (single line, 88 chars — fine without a lint gate).

Now **Change 2** — the app-mode mail e2e. Where: the reviewer suggested extending `panel-app.spec.ts`. But the coverage gap is about mail visibility/dedup — `mail-visible.spec.ts` is the mail spec. Hmm. The thread: "e.g. extend `panel-app.spec.ts` to drive mail via the SW `pushMail` seam and assert the dedup-by-message-id invariant through an app-side hook". I'll add it to mail-visible.spec.ts as a third test — it's the spec that owns #320 coverage; reply explains placement. Actually, let me reconsider: the reviewer's example names panel-app.spec.ts. But placing an app-surface mail test in mail-visible.spec.ts keeps every mail assertion in one file and both files' describe blocks clean. Substance over the example's letter. I'll go with mail-visible.spec.ts.

Test design:

```ts
test('AC18 app-surface: the --with-app leg sees mail exactly once over the '
    + 'ui-v2 wire the Flutter app consumes (#1521 coverage gap)', async ({ fa }) => {
  test.skip(
    !appBundlePresent,
    'needs the --with-app build (browser_ext/panel/app/ missing)',
  );
  await fa.bootAgent('unattended');
  // The Flutter app subscribes over a chrome.runtime port named
  // 'fa-ui-v2' (SW UI port server); the canvas renders nothing a DOM
  // probe can read, so the test adopts the app's own wire: a probe port
  // on the panel page collects the exact envelopes the app renders from.
  await fa.panel.evaluate(() => {
    const w = window as unknown as {
      __faProbe: { msgs: unknown[] };
      chrome: { runtime: { connect(ci: { name: string }): { onMessage: { addListener(cb: (m: unknown) => void): void };
    };
    const probe = { msgs: [] as unknown[] };
    const port = w.chrome.runtime.connect({ name: 'fa-ui-v2' });
    port.onMessage.addListener((m) => probe.msgs.push(m));
    w.__faProbe = probe;
  });
  const probeMsgs = () =>
    fa.panel.evaluate(() => (window as unknown as { __faProbe: { msgs: Record<string, unknown>[] }).__faProbe.msgs);

  // Same delivery twice (at-least-once hub) — AC18 dedup-by-message-id.
  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    await fa.swEval((payload: [string, string]) => {
      const sw = globalThis as unknown as {
        faAgent: { pushMail(from: string, text: string): void };
      };
      sw.faAgent.pushMail(payload[0], payload[1]);
    }, ['peer-app', MAIL_APP]);
  }
  // The turn the FIRST delivery started lands: exactly one attributed
  // user row and one assistant reply on the app wire.
  await expect
    .poll(async () => {
      const msgs = await probeMsgs();
      return msgs.filter(
        (m) =>
          m.kind === 'message_done' &&
          (m.message as { role?: string }).role === 'user' &&
          (m.message as { text?: string }).text === MAIL_ROW_APP,
      ).length;
    })
    .toBe(1);
  await expect
    .poll(async () => {
      const msgs = await probeMsgs();
      return msgs.filter(
        (m) =>
          m.kind === 'message_done' &&
          (m.message as { role?: string }).role === 'assistant',
      ).length;
    })
    .toBe(1);
  // Settle past any would-be duplicate turn, then re-assert: still one.
  await fa.panel.waitForTimeout(1500);
  const msgs = await probeMsgs();
  expect(msgs.filter(...user...).length).toBe(1);
});
```

Wait — a detail: pushing the same mail twice back-to-back. First pushMail starts a turn (`_runTurn`). Second pushMail is deduped (same from+text) — dropped. So exactly one turn. 

But there's a race subtlety: two identical pushes; deduper keyed on from+text, not message id. AC18 invariant = "dedup-by-message-id" (conceptually; implementation keys from+text). Fine.

Another subtlety: does the user message_done arrive on the ui-v2 wire before or... The host emits user message_done at turn start (message_done role user), then assistant streams + message_done assistant. Poll handles ordering.

Also the wire envelope: `{kind:'message_done', message:{role, text, ...}}` — `_translate` removes 'type' from the event and spreads the rest, so message has role/text.

One more consideration: boot — in app mode, does booting need a reload for the app to attach? Irrelevant for our probe: we connect after boot.

Also: does `fa.panel.evaluate` chrome.runtime.connect work in the app iframe page? The app page is at chrome-extension://id/panel/app/index.html — extension origin, chrome.runtime available. Yes.

MV3 keepalive: our probe port keeps the SW alive. Also fa.swEval re-wakes as needed.

Potential issue: `await fa.bootAgent('unattended')` — in the legacy spec they boot then reload panel because the panel push port may be orphaned. In app mode the Flutter app's own port may be orphaned but that doesn't affect our assertions.

Also check: does pushMail exist on the seam used before boot? We boot first. Good.

Also, should this test also cover the mid-run pending indicator on the app wire (steer indicator analog)? The thread mentions the steer indicator too. Keep surgical: mail visibility + dedup. I'll note in the reply that the steer indicator app-surface analog remains unit-covered; extending this probe pattern to steer is a possible follow-up. Hmm — but "every open thread needs a code fix" — one solid code fix per thread suffices.

Now, RED verification: the "issue" was absence of the test. To follow TDD pragmatically: 
1. Write test.
2. Build with app, run mail-visible in app mode → new test should PASS (it's new coverage; there's no failing state before since it didn't exist). 

Actually a genuine RED check: before this fix, the app leg had no such test — nothing to run. I'll demonstrate RED differently: run the full app leg before my change? That just shows the two legacy tests skip. The meaningful verification is GREEN on app leg + legacy leg unaffected. I'll do that.

Environment steps:
1. Make edits.
2. Install e2e deps: `cd browser_ext/e2e && npm ci` (package-lock exists) — background.
3. Build agent.js: globalSetup does it, or run build script (but build script also makes zip; globalSetup runs the script without --with-app, which builds agent.js and packages zip — a bit heavy but fine). Actually for legacy leg we can run globalSetup path.
4. Legacy leg: run steer-stream + mail-visible specs (should pass: mail AC1/AC6 now skipped? No wait — legacy leg has NO app bundle, so skips don't fire and all 6 tests run and pass).
5. App leg: `bash scripts/build_browser_ext.sh --with-app` (background, long), then run mail-visible + steer-stream + panel-app in app mode.

Also I must conclude the in-progress merge commit at the end (the staged workflow change). And write outputs.

